#lg 2.154	proverb

@(B.2.154.39 = 39)  @note{= Alster 1997 2.154 = 6.2; cf. 5.6.1: l. 94} The palace is a huge river; its interior is a goring bull.

#lg 2.155	proverb

@(B.2.155.40 = 40-42)  @note{= Alster 1997 2.155 = 6.3; cf. @xref[t.6.1.28,t.6.1.28.24]{6.1.28.24}, @xref[t.6.2.3,]{6.2.3: UET 6/2 209 ll. 1-3}, @xref[t.1.8.1.5,]{1.8.1.5: ll. 193-199}} The palace is a forest. The king is a lion. @wDN{Nungal} overwhelms men with a huge battle-net. Oh @wDN{Utu}, accept my prayer.

#lg 2.156	proverb

@(B.2.156.43 = 43)  @note{= Alster 1997 2.156 = 6.4; cf. @xref[t.6.1.14,t.6.1.14.23]{6.1.14.23}, @xref[t.6.1.25,t.6.1.25.8]{6.1.25.8}} The palace is a slippery place which catches those who do not know it.

#lg 2.157	proverb

@(B.2.157.44 = 44-47)  @note{= Alster 1997 2.157 = 6.5; cf. @xref[t.6.1.14,t.6.1.14.20]{6.1.14.20}, @xref[t.6.1.25,t.6.1.25.11]{6.1.25.11}, @xref[t.6.2.3,t623.h27]{6.2.3: UET 6/2 317}} The palace cannot avoid the waste land. A barge cannot avoid straw. A freeborn man cannot avoid corvée work. A king's daughter cannot avoid the tavern.

#lg 2.158	proverb

@(B.2.158.48 = 48-49)  @note{= Alster 1997 2.158 = 6.6; cf. @xref[t.6.1.14,t.6.1.14.22]{6.1.14.22}, @xref[t.6.1.17,t.6.1.17.b9]{6.1.17.b9}, @xref[t.6.1.25,t.6.1.25.10]{6.1.25.10}} The palace -- one day a mother giving birth, the next day a lamenting mother.

#lg 2.159	proverb

